<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 8: Handling Missing Values                               -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Opens with a real, verified story: Abraham Wald and the           -->
<!--  Statistical Research Group's WWII aircraft-survivability work.    -->
<!--  Chosen deliberately over a "disaster" story (unlike ch2/ch4):     -->
<!--  it is the classic case of reasoning correctly, and it is exactly  -->
<!--  about the imputation/deletion-adjacent question this chapter      -->
<!--  needs (why is data missing, and what does that imply about how    -->
<!--  to treat it), which none of the ch4 stories (inconsistency,       -->
<!--  duplication, silent recoding) actually covered.                  -->
<!--  Verified via multiple independent searches before writing:       -->
<!--    - Wald, A. (1943). "A Method of Estimating Plane Vulnerability  -->
<!--      Based on Damage of Survivors." Statistical Research Group,    -->
<!--      Columbia University, memorandum for the National Defense     -->
<!--      Research Committee (reprinted, Center for Naval Analyses,    -->
<!--      1980).                                                       -->
<!--    - Mangel, M. and Samaniego, F.J. (1984). "Abraham Wald's Work   -->
<!--      on Aircraft Survivability." Journal of the American           -->
<!--      Statistical Association, 79(386), 259-267. Peer-reviewed      -->
<!--      formalisation of Wald's original (classified at the time)     -->
<!--      memoranda - used as the authoritative secondary source for    -->
<!--      what his method actually did technically, specifically to    -->
<!--      avoid the oversimplified pop-science "just armour the         -->
<!--      engines" version. His actual method: assume hits are roughly  -->
<!--      uniformly distributed over the airframe by surface area, then -->
<!--      use returning-plane damage counts to infer conditional        -->
<!--      survival/vulnerability by section, explicitly correcting for  -->
<!--      the missing (non-returning) planes rather than reading        -->
<!--      returning-plane damage at face value.                        -->
<!--  MCAR/MAR/MNAR formal definitions and the na.rm/complete.cases     -->
<!--  material are adapted from the old, unbuilt drafts                -->
<!--  (ch6_descriptive_statistics.ptx sec-missing-data, and             -->
<!--  ch6_description_2.ptx subsec-missing-formal), rewritten to open   -->
<!--  with Wald as the MNAR example rather than presenting the taxonomy -->
<!--  cold.                                                             -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-handling-missing-values" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Handling Missing Values</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-handling-missing-values">
    <p>
      In <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>, we ran into <c>NA</c> as soon as we
      tried to calculate a mean, and set the problem aside.
      In <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, we looked at the gaps, and started
      to think informally about why VARIBABLES specifically had NAs.
    </p>

    <p>
      The big idea in this chapter is easy to say, and easy to underestimate:
      <em>why</em> a value is missing decides what you should do about it.
      Sometimes missing values are just background noise, and you can safely
      ignore them.
      Sometimes they're the most important information in the whole dataset.
      The study below is one where working out that difference really mattered.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: Wald and the bombers                                  -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-intro-missing-values">
    <title>The Patients Who Aren't in the Data</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, the problem was ...
        .
        This case study is about [..]: data that should have existed, but simply
        isn't there at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        Adhiambo et al. (2025) followed adults getting HIV treatment at five
        health facilities in western Kenya, and asked a question that sounds
        simple: how many of them had missed a routine blood test they were
        supposed to have?
        The answer turned out to depend completely on working out why each
        person was missing, and not just how many were.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-background">
      <title>A test that tells you if treatment is working</title>

      <p>
        People living with HIV take antiretroviral therapy (ART) to keep the
        virus suppressed, ideally to levels so low that it can't be transmitted
        and can't seriously damage the body.
        The most direct way to know whether that's actually happening for a
        given patient is a <term>viral load</term> test: a blood test that
        measures how much virus is circulating.
        <fn>
    It isn't the only way. Before viral load testing became routine, clinics
    relied on CD4 cell counts (a proxy that tracks immune system damage) and
    on watching for clinical symptoms of disease progression. Both work, but
    both lag behind. Viral load can rise for months before CD4 count falls or
    symptoms appear, so a patient whose treatment is failing may go
    undetected for a long time under either older method. That's why viral
    load testing was treated as such a major improvement when it was
    introduced.
  </fn>
        Kenyan national guidelines, in line with World Health Organization
        recommendations, say this test should happen 6 and 12 months after
        starting treatment, and every 12 months after that.
      </p>

      <p>
        The first year is when treatment failure is most likely, and a timely
        viral load result is what lets a clinician catch it early, before drug
        resistance develops or the patient's health declines further.
        A missed viral load test could mean a patient has failing treatment,
        with nobody currently able to tell.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-study">
      <title>Following 1,754 people for two years</title>

      <p>
        The study sat inside a larger clinical trial running from 2014 to 2019,
        across three urban clinics in Kisumu County and two more rural clinics
        in Migori County.
        Viral load monitoring wasn't managed specially for the trial.
        It happened through ordinary, routine clinic care, which is exactly what
        makes the results useful: they show what actually happens in the health
        system, not what happens under ideal research conditions.
      </p>

      <p>
        For every one of 1,754 participants, the researchers checked whether a
        viral load result existed in the 9 to 15 month window after starting
        treatment, and again in the 21 to 27 month window.
        If a result couldn't be found in the routine records, that wasn't the
        end of the process.
        The researchers went further: checking laboratory logs, a national
        database, paper files, and eventually contacting clinicians directly, to
        work out, case by case, what had actually happened to that missing test.
        Later in this chapter, we'll come back to why that second step mattered
        so much.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-headline-number">
      <title>The number you'd report first</title>

      <p>
        In year one, 428 of 1,754 participants, 24.4%, had no viral load result
        in their window.
        In year two, it was 502 of 1,709, 29.4%.
        Almost a third of patients, on the most basic reading of the data,
        simply didn't have the information their doctors needed.
      </p>

      <p>
        Faced with a gap like this, the easiest thing to do is to just work with
        the data you have.
        Take the roughly 1,300 people who do have a year-one viral load result,
        calculate whatever you need from them, (look at `urban vs rural`, `older
        vs younger`), and assume it applies to everyone.
      </p>

      <p>
        Faced with a gap like this, the easiest thing to do is to just work with
        what you have.
        Take the roughly 1,300 people who do have a year-one viral load result,
        calculate whatever you need from them, urban vs rural, older vs younger,
        and assume it applies to everyone.
      </p>

      <p>
        Think about a class of 100 students sitting an exam, where 24 are absent
        on the day.
        If you calculate the class average from the 76 who showed up, is that a
        fair estimate of how the whole class of 100 would have done?
        It depends entirely on <em>why</em> those 24 weren't there.
        If they were kept home by 24 unrelated colds and stomach bugs, there's
        no reason to think they'd have scored any differently, so the average
        from the 76 is a perfectly good stand-in for all 100.
        But if most of the 24 stayed home because they hadn't done the reading
        and didn't want to sit the exam, the 76 who showed up are, on average,
        the more prepared half of the class.
        Their average isn't a fair estimate of everyone anymore.
        It's an estimate of the students who choose to turn up when they're
        ready.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same question applies to the 428 people missing a year-one viral
        load.
        Working with just the 1,300 who were tested is only safe if <em>why</em>
        someone went untested has nothing to do with whether their treatment was
        working.
        In this dataset, it turns out that isn't the case.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-why">
      <title>Ah, but why is it missing?</title>

      <p>
        The extended investigation gave the missing results a reason, and the
        reasons were not evenly spread.
        In year one, of the 406 participants who were still alive and missing a
        test, 149 (36.7%) were missing because a clinician misread the
        monitoring schedule, ordering the next test 12 months after the
        <em>last</em> viral load rather than 12 months after treatment started.
        209 (51.5%) had missed every clinic appointment during the testing
        window and couldn't be found at all.
        42 (10.3%) had no documented reason.
        And 6 (1.5%) had a test ordered by a clinician, with no record that a
        sample was ever collected.
      </p>

      <p>
        Four reasons, and as we'll see, each one changes what the missing data
        is quietly telling you.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-mcar">
      <title>Some people are missing for no reason at all</title>

      <p>
        There are 6 participants in year one, and 6 again in year two, whose
        clinician ordered a viral load test, and where a sample was, apparently,
        simply never collected.
        No record of a patient refusal.
        No record of a clinical decision.
        Just a test that should have happened, and didn't, for reasons that look
        like nothing more than an administrative slip.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the closest thing in the dataset to missingness that has nothing
        to do with the patient at all.
        It's the "kept home by an unrelated cold" case from our exam: whatever
        happened to these six people, it wasn't connected to whether their
        treatment was working.
        Losing these six records doesn't quietly shift your results in any
        particular direction.
        There's no reason to think they'd have looked any different from the
        patients who were tested, so leaving them out costs you six data points,
        and nothing else.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-mar">
      <title>Some people are missing because of who they are</title>

      <p>
        Two other characteristics also predicted missingness, but in a different
        way.
        Participants under 24 had more than double the risk of a missed viral
        load compared to those over 44, and participants at rural clinics had a
        higher risk than those at urban clinics.
        Neither of these is a measurement of how sick someone is.
        They're just facts about the patient that happen to be recorded already,
        in every participant's file, tested or not.
      </p>

      <p>
        That difference matters.
        Can we assume that younger and older patients would show the same viral
        suppression results, if we could see everyone's?
        Not necessarily; the paper suggests younger patients are more mobile,
        and more likely to fall out of care for reasons that have nothing to do
        with how their treatment is going.
        Can we assume rural and urban patients would look the same?
        Again, probably not exactly, since rural clinics face harder equipment
        access and longer sample transport.
        But because age and clinic location are known for every single
        participant, missing or not, they at least give you something to work
        with.
        You can compare rural patients to rural patients, and under-24s to
        under-24s, instead of guessing at the group as a whole.
        A gap you can explain using information you already have!
        But, assuming these were
      </p>

      <p>
        That difference matters.
        Unlike the six lost samples, we can't just assume these absences were
        unrelated to anything, the way we could with an unlucky cold.
        Think back to the exam: imagine the 24 absent students weren't a random
        slice of the class, but were disproportionately the students who come to
        school from a further, poorer part of town, and they didn't make it in
        that day.
        Averaging just the students who made it in would quietly overrepresent
        those who live closer, and you'd have no way to know it, unless you'd
        also recorded where each student lived.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's the difference here.
        Age and clinic location aren't a guess.
        They're recorded for every single participant, tested or not.
        So instead of assuming the untested under-24s would have looked like the
        tested over-44s, or that untested rural patients would have looked like
        tested urban ones, we can do something more careful: compare like with
        like.
        Look at suppression rates among tested under-24s, and treat that as your
        best estimate for untested under-24s, rather than borrowing an estimate
        from a different age group entirely.
        A gap you can explain using information you already have is a very
        different problem from a gap you can only explain using the very thing
        you're trying to measure, which is exactly the case we turn to next.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-mnar">
      <title>And some people are missing because they're sicker</title>

      <p>
        The study's analysis found that participants at the most advanced stage
        of HIV disease (WHO clinical stage 4), had more than twice the risk of
        missing their year-two viral load compared to those at stage 1.
        The research team also learned something that never made it into any
        official record at all: some clinicians told them, when asked directly,
        that they had deliberately avoided sending viral load tests for patients
        they suspected were already failing treatment, because they already
        knew, or believed, the result would come back high.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the most dangerous kind of missing data there is, because the
        reason for the gap is tangled up with the very thing you're trying to
        measure.
        Go back to the exam one last time.
        This is the student who stayed home because they hadn't done the reading
        and didn't want to sit a test they knew they'd fail.
        Their absence isn't unrelated to their score, like the cold, and it
        isn't something you could correct for with a note about which bus they
        take, like the traffic.
        Their absence <em>is</em> their score, in the sense that not showing up
        is itself evidence of how unprepared they were.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same is true here.
        The patients missing from the "viral load" column aren't a random slice
        of everyone, and they aren't a slice you can identify from something
        else in their file, the way you could identify an under-24 or a rural
        patient.
        They're disproportionately the patients doing worst, and the very fact
        of their disease progressing is bound up with why they weren't tested.
        If you calculate what fraction of tested patients are virally suppressed
        and treat that as the answer for everyone, you'll come out too
        optimistic, because the sickest people were the ones most likely never
        to be tested in the first place.
        There's no group to compare like with like here, the way there was for
        age or clinic location.
        The information you'd need to correct for the gap is the same
        information that's missing.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-lesson">
      <title>Missingness is not one problem</title>

      <p>
        Put these different reasons side by side, and you can see why treating
        "24.4% missing" as a single fact would have been a mistake.
        Lost to follow-up, which accounts for over half of all missing cases,
        could mean a death that was never recorded, a self-transfer to another
        clinic, or a patient who is very much alive and simply out of care.
        Clinician misinterpretation of the guidelines, more than a third,
        reflects a scheduling error, unrelated to the patient's health at all.
        Advanced disease stage reflects the sickest patients disappearing from
        the very data used to judge how well treatment is working.
        And the handful of lost samples reflect nothing more than bad luck.
      </p>

      <p>
        Adhiambo et al.
        (2025) are explicit that this changes what the topline numbers can be
        trusted to say.
        Missed viral load monitoring, they write,
        <q>remains high, primarily due to loss to follow-up, and may
        substantially alter suppression estimates.</q>
        Once the team understood that the missing data wasn't random, they
        stopped relying on the case database for routine reporting and used
        laboratory data for day-to-day reports instead.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's the habit this chapter is trying to build.
        A missing value is never just an empty cell.
        Before you decide whether to drop it, fill it in, or flag it and move
        on, you have to ask why it's empty.
        Sometimes that question has a simple answer, and you can safely ignore
        the gap.
        Sometimes it has an answer tied to information you already have
        elsewhere, and you can work around it.
        And sometimes, as with the sickest patients in this study, the answer is
        tied to the exact thing you were trying to measure, and ignoring it
        could seriously bias your study.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Adhiambo, H.F., Lewis-Kulzer, J., Nyagesoa, E.,
        Gimbel, S., Akama, E., Mangale, D., Montonya, L., Eshun-Wilson, I.,
        Iguna, S., Nyandieka, E., Bukusi, E., Abuogi, L., Odeny, T., Petersen,
        M.L.
        and Geng, E.H.
        (2025).
        <q>Examining and classifying reasons for missing viral loads among
        adults living with HIV: An extended outcome investigation and
        ascertainment approach in Western Kenya.</q>
        <em>PLOS Global Public Health</em>, 5(5), e0004038.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch8-stack">
    <title>STACK question</title>

    <p>
      TODO: add STACK question
    </p>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: applied to the flamingo data                          -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-missing-applied">
    <title>Handling Missing Values, Applied</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let's go back to the flamingo data, and the gaps we found in <xref
        ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>: 4 missing bill depths, 8 missing masses,
        and 7 missing sexes.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        colSums(is.na(kenya_flamingos))

        #&gt;           lake        species bill_length_mm  bill_depth_mm  leg_length_mm
        #&gt;              0              0              0              4              0
        #&gt;    body_mass_g            sex           year
        #&gt;              8              7              0
        </code>
      </program>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-flamingo-mechanism">
      <title>Which pattern applies to which gap</title>

      <p>
        In <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, we sketched out some possible
        reasons for these gaps.
        Now we can give them proper names:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Say the battery in the scale ran out mid-morning, so every mass
              after that point went unrecorded, however heavy the bird was.
              That's roughly <term>MCAR</term> (missing completely at random),
              as long as nothing about which birds happened to be weighed that
              morning is linked to their mass.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Working out a bird's sex means handling it more closely, and the
              field team only does that with calmer birds.
              If being <q>calm enough to handle closely</q> isn't linked to any
              of the bird's other recorded traits, this could be MCAR.
              But if calmer birds tend to be a particular species or size, which
              the dataset does record, it becomes <term>MAR</term> (missing at
              random) instead.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              But what if the heaviest, strongest birds are the ones that
              struggle free before anyone can weigh them?
              That's <term>MNAR</term> (missing not at random): whether a mass
              is missing depends on the mass itself.
              It's exactly the same structure as the sickest patients missing
              their viral load tests, and it means the 336 recorded masses
              aren't a fair sample of the true weights of all 344 birds.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The dataset on its own can't tell you which of these is true.
        That has to come from knowing how the data was collected.
        It's the same conclusion we came to in <xref
        ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, but now we have the words to say exactly
        what's at stake in each case.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-na-rm-precise">
      <title>What <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> actually does</title>

      <p>
        It removes the missing observations, and does the calculation on
        whatever's left.
        Put simply: <em>you're no longer analysing the sample you thought you
        were.</em>
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        mean(kenya_flamingos$body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 1957.405
        </code>
      </program>
      <p>
        This is the mean of 336 birds, and not 344.
        And it's really important to be clear that <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> is
        <em>not</em> imputation.
        It doesn't estimate the missing values.
        It just leaves those birds out of the calculation completely, and it
        does it silently, unless you check <c>sum(!is.na(...))</c> yourself.
        If the missing values are MCAR, leaving them out costs you some
        precision, and nothing else.
        But if they're MNAR, it can make your answer wrong, in a direction you
        can't detect from the 336 rows you have left.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-complete-cases-applied">
      <title>Complete cases and listwise deletion</title>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        sum(complete.cases(kenya_flamingos))
        #&gt; [1] 325

        nrow(kenya_flamingos)
        #&gt; [1] 344
        </code>
      </program>
      <p>
        Only 325 of the 344 rows are complete.
        That means 19 rows (5.5%) have at least one missing value somewhere,
        which is more than the 8 missing masses on their own, because the gaps
        for different variables fall in different rows.
        Dropping every row with a gap (<term>listwise
        deletion</term><idx><h>listwise deletion</h></idx>) is convenient, and
        keeps every analysis on exactly the same sample.
        But it throws away data you don't need to lose: a bird that's only
        missing <c>sex</c> still has a perfectly good mass measurement.
        For descriptive work, we'd recommend using <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> one
        variable at a time, instead of blanket listwise deletion, and reporting
        the <m>n</m> for each.
      </p>

      <remark xml:id="remark-report-n-ch8">
        <title>Report the n</title>

        <p>
          Never report a mean, a standard deviation, or any other summary
          without also saying the <m>n</m> it was calculated from.
          A summary of 344 birds, 336 birds, or 325 birds is a different claim
          about a different sample, and a table without <m>n</m> makes it
          impossible to tell them apart.
        </p>
      </remark>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-imputation-caution">
      <title>Imputation is a claim about your data</title>

      <p>
        A common third option, alongside leaving values out with <c>na.rm</c>
        and full listwise deletion, is
        <term>imputation</term><idx><h>imputation</h></idx>: filling in each
        missing value with an estimate, often the variable's mean or a
        prediction from a model, so you don't need to drop any rows at all.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        mass_imputed &lt;- ifelse(
          is.na(kenya_flamingos$body_mass_g),
          mean(kenya_flamingos$body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE),
          kenya_flamingos$body_mass_g
        )
        </code>
      </program>
      <p>
        This line runs without any complaints, and gives you a column with no
        missing values at all.
        But if the missing values are MNAR, it's actually worse than doing
        nothing.
        It leaves the bias in place and hides it, replacing 8 honestly missing
        values with 8 confident-looking numbers, built from the very mean that
        the bias has already distorted.
        Imputation is a reasonable tool when values are MCAR or MAR, where the
        data you have really does contain the information you need to make a
        sensible estimate.
        When values are MNAR, you need an outside model of <em>why</em> the data
        is missing (like Wald's assumption about where bombers get hit, in the
        exercises at the end of this chapter), and a one-line formula applied to
        whatever happens to be left won't do that.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-handling-missing-values">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-wald-survivorship">
      <title>The Bombers That Didn't Come Back</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          During the Second World War, the US military looked at bombers coming
          back from missions over Germany, and recorded where each one had been
          hit.
          The damage wasn't evenly spread.
          There was far more of it on the wings and fuselage than on the
          engines.
          The obvious recommendation followed straight away: reinforce the wings
          and fuselage, since that's clearly where the bullets go.
        </p>

        <p>
          The mathematician Abraham Wald was working with a team the US military
          had put together to bring statistics to exactly this kind of problem,
          and he disagreed.
          The damage counts were almost certainly accurate.
          His objection was about which planes had been counted at all.
        </p>

        <p>
          Every bomber in that dataset had survived its mission and made it
          home.
          A bomber that was hit badly enough in the engine didn't come back to
          be inspected, photographed, and added to the tally.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-a">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Whose damage is missing from this dataset, and why?
            Think about what has to be true of a bomber for it to end up
            recorded at all.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            The planes missing from the dataset are the ones that were shot
            down, or damaged so badly that they never made it back to base.
            A plane only gets inspected and added to the tally if it survives
            the mission and lands, so the dataset only ever tells you about
            <q>damage a plane can survive</q>, and never about <q>damage that
            brings a plane down</q>.
            Whether a plane appears in the data at all depends directly on how
            badly it was hit, and where.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-b">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The engines show very little damage in the data.
            Give two different explanations for that pattern, one where the
            engines really were rarely hit, and one where they were hit just as
            often as anywhere else.
            What would have to be true of the missing planes for each
            explanation to be correct?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            <term>Explanation one: engines really were rarely hit.</term> This
            would be true if enemy fire, for whatever reason, tended to miss the
            engines and land on the wings and fuselage instead, perhaps because
            of the plane's shape, flight path, or the positioning of gunners.
            Under this explanation, the missing planes (the ones that didn't
            come back) would show roughly the same pattern as the surviving
            planes: mostly wing and fuselage hits, occasionally an engine hit.
          </p>

          <p>
            <term>Explanation two: engines were hit just as often, but rarely
      survived.</term>
            This would be true if hits were spread fairly evenly across the
            whole aircraft, but a hit to the engine was usually fatal to the
            mission, while a hit to the wing or fuselage usually wasn't.
            Under this explanation, the missing planes would show a very
            different pattern from the surviving planes: heavily concentrated on
            the engines, which is exactly why none of them are in the data to
            show it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The dataset alone can't distinguish between these two explanations,
            because it only contains the survivors either way.
            Wald's contribution was recognising that the second explanation was
            far more plausible on engineering grounds, and building a method
            that assumed it.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-c">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose you simply excluded any plane you had no data for, in this
            case, this is every plane that never returned, and analysed only the
            bombers that came back.
            Would this be safe?
            Compare this to a case where a few data logs were lost to a filing
            error unrelated to how damaged the plane was.
            Why does one gap sink the analysis and the other doesn't?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            It would not be safe, and it's already exactly what happened, since
            the data was never gathered on the planes that didn't return in the
            first place.
            The problem is that whether a plane is missing is tied directly to
            how much damage it took, and specifically where.
            Missing planes aren't a random subset of all planes.
            They're the worst-damaged ones, concentrated in one part of the
            aircraft.
            Removing them (or, as happened, never having them) removes exactly
            the evidence you'd need to see the true pattern.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare that to a handful of paperwork logs lost in a filing error.
            If which logs get lost has nothing to do with where the plane was
            hit, some records for lightly-damaged planes and some for
            heavily-damaged planes will disappear at roughly the same rate.
            The planes you can still see remain a fair picture of all planes,
            just a slightly smaller one.
            One kind of gap changes what conclusion you'd draw.
            The other just costs you a bit of precision.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-d">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Wald's actual recommendation was to reinforce the sections that were
            <em>least</em> represented in the damage data, not the most.
            Using your answer to <xref ref="ex-wald-a"/>, explain why that's the
            opposite conclusion from the obvious one, and why it's the correct
            one.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            The obvious recommendation treats heavy damage in the data as a sign
            of a weak spot: <q>we see a lot of wing damage, so wings must be
            vulnerable, so let's reinforce them.</q>
            But because the data only contains survivors, lots of visible damage
            to a section is actually evidence that the section can take a hit
            and keep flying.
            It's a sign of strength.
          </p>

          <p>
            A section that's barely damaged in the data, like the engines, isn't
            necessarily a section that rarely gets hit.
            Given that hits are spread fairly evenly over the whole airframe,
            its absence from the data is far better explained by planes hit
            there not surviving to be counted.
            So the section with the least visible damage is the one hiding the
            planes you never got to see, which is exactly the section that most
            needs reinforcing.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-e">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Could you fix this problem by simply gathering more data, for
            example, inspecting even more returning bombers?
            Why or why not?
            What kind of missing data can be fixed by collecting more of the
            same kind of records, and what kind can't?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            No.
            Inspecting more returning bombers only gives you more survivors, and
            survivors are exactly the group that's already overrepresented.
            No matter how many more of them you inspect, you'll never see the
            damage pattern on a plane that didn't make it back, because those
            planes are permanently absent from the pool you're sampling from.
            More data of the same kind just makes you more confident in the
            wrong picture.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is the key difference from the filing-error case in
            <xref ref="ex-wald-c"/>.
            If your gap is caused by something unrelated to the thing you're
            measuring, like a lost log, collecting more records genuinely helps,
            because the new records aren't systematically excluding a whole
            category of outcome.
            But if the gap exists <em>because</em> of the thing you're
            measuring, like a plane not surviving because of where it was hit,
            no amount of additional data collected the same way will ever
            recover it.
            You need a different source of information entirely (in Wald's case,
            the logic of the missingness itself) rather than more of what you
            already have.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-challenger-missing">
      <title>The Launches That Weren't on the Chart</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          In <xref ref="ex-challenger"/> we looked at the night before the
          Challenger launch in January 1986. Engineers at Morton Thiokol, the
          company that built the rocket boosters, argued the launch should be
          delayed because the O-ring seals could fail in the cold. NASA went
          ahead, and all seven crew members were killed.
        </p>

        <p>
          Edward Tufte's Challenger graph is a famous case study in data
          visualisation. It shows how a scatter plot of O-ring damage against
          launch temperature could have made the dangers of the launch really
          clear. In his book <em>Visual Explanations</em> (1997), Tufte argued
          that the disaster was partly a failure of data communication: the
          engineers had the evidence, but it wasn't shown in a way that let
          anyone see the pattern.
        </p>

        <p>
          Tufte's argument, and our exercise, focused on how the data was shown. But there's
          another problem with the charts the engineers used, and it's the same
          kind of problem as Wald's bombers. When they looked at whether
          temperature mattered, they only included launches where O-ring damage
          had been found. The launches with no damage at all weren't there.
        </p>

        <p>
          We use the same <c>orings</c> data from the <c>DAAG</c> package.
          Each row is one launch before Challenger, <c>Temperature</c> is the
          launch temperature in degrees Fahrenheit, and <c>Total</c> is the
          number of O-rings showing damage.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
            library(DAAG)
            library(dplyr)
            library(ggplot2)
          </code>
        </program>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-missing-a">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Filter <c>orings</c> down to the launches where <c>Total &gt; 0</c>,
            which is what the engineers' charts showed. How many launches are
            in the full data, and how many are left after filtering?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <program language="r">
            <code>
              orings_damaged &lt;- orings %&gt;%
                filter(Total &gt; 0)

              nrow(orings)
              nrow(orings_damaged)
            </code>
          </program>
          <p>
            Most of the launches disappear. Only the minority with some damage
            are left, so any chart built from <c>orings_damaged</c> is
            describing a much smaller, and very particular, group of launches.
            This is exactly why we say to always report the <m>n</m>
            (<xref ref="remark-report-n-ch8"/>).
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-missing-b">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Plot <c>Temperature</c> against <c>Total</c> twice: once for all
            launches, and once for the damaged launches only. Add the forecast
            temperature for the Challenger launch (26 to 29°F) as a shaded
            region, as you did in <xref ref="ex-challenger-b"/>. How does the
            relationship between temperature and damage change when the
            zero-damage launches are left out?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <program language="r">
            <code>
              ggplot(orings_damaged, aes(x = Temperature, y = Total)) +
                geom_point() +
                xlim(25, 85) +
                ylim(0, 6) +
                annotate("rect",
                         xmin = 26, xmax = 29,
                         ymin = 0,  ymax = 6,
                         alpha = 0.3, fill = "red") +
                annotate("text",
                         x = 33, y = 3,
                         label = "Forecast\ntemperature\n26 to 29°F",
                         hjust = 0) +
                labs(x = "Temperature (°F)",
                     y = "Total O-ring damage",
                     title = "O-ring damage by launch temperature",
                     subtitle = "Launches with damage only: zero-damage launches excluded") +
                theme_bw()
            </code>
          </program>
          <p>
            For the full data, swap <c>orings_damaged</c> for <c>orings</c>
            and change the subtitle.
          </p>
          <p>
            With every launch included, the pattern is clear: the cold launches
            all had damage, and nearly all the damage-free launches happened at
            warmer temperatures. Take the zero-damage launches away and that
            pattern really fades. What's left is damage scattered across a
            range of temperatures, and it looks like damage can happen whether
            it's cold or warm. The launches that were left out were the ones
            that showed warm launches tend to go fine, and that was the
            evidence that cold was the problem.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-missing-c">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What decided whether a launch ended up on the chart? Were the
            launches that got left out just a random handful, or was there
            something about them that made them go missing? How does that
            compare to Wald's bombers?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            It wasn't random at all. Whether a launch was included depended
            on its value of <c>Total</c>: launches with zero damage were
            dropped, and launches with some damage were kept. So the launches
            went missing because of the very thing we were trying to measure.
            That's the same structure as the bombers, where whether a plane was
            in the data depended on how badly it had been hit. And just like
            the bombers, it means the data that's left gives us a distorted
            picture, not just a smaller one.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-missing-d">
        <statement>
          <p>
            In <xref ref="ex-wald-e"/> we saw that no amount of extra data
            collected the same way could fix Wald's problem. Is the Challenger
            case the same? What would it have taken to fix it?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            No, and this is what makes the Challenger case so hard to look at.
            Wald's missing planes were lost over enemy territory, and nobody
            could ever inspect them. The zero-damage launches were never
            missing from the records. The engineers had that data; it just
            didn't make it onto the chart. The fix didn't need new data or a
            clever model of the missingness. It needed someone to ask
            <q>which launches are we not looking at, and why?</q>
          </p>
          <p>
            Missing data isn't only something that happens to us during data
            collection. We can also create it ourselves, with a filter or a
            decision about what's <q>relevant</q>, and it can do just as much
            damage.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <conclusion>
        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Tufte, E.R. (1997).
          <em>Visual Explanations: Images and Quantities, Evidence and
          Narrative.</em> Graphics Press. Data from the <c>DAAG</c> R package.
          Case study adapted from Helveston, J.P. (2019), George Washington
          University,
          <url href="https://p4a.seas.gwu.edu/2019-Fall/challengerExplosion.html"/>,
          CC BY 4.0.
        </p>
      </conclusion>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-missing-imputation-risk">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the code in <xref ref="ss-imputation-caution"/> as a starting
          point, explain what would happen to the mean and the standard
          deviation of <c>body_mass_g</c> if you used <c>mass_imputed</c>,
          instead of the original column with <c>na.rm = TRUE</c>.
          Which one changes, which one doesn't, and why does that matter for
          anyone reading a report built on this column?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>